.TH OSFP\-BASELINE 1 2026-09-23 osfp "User Commands"
.SH NAME
osfp\-baseline \- record a fingerprint of the filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B osfp baseline
.RB [ \-o
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-exclude
.IR PATH ]...\&
.RB [ \-\-exclude\-from
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-default\-excludes ]
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-mounts
.IR POLICY ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-depth
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-file\-size
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-jobs
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-os\-id
.IR KEY ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-allow\-non\-root ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B osfp baseline
walks the filesystem from the root, records the path, type, mode, owner,
group, size and modification time of every entry, hashes every regular file
with SHA\-256, reads the target of every symbolic link, and writes the result
to a fingerprint file.
It must run as root.
.PP
The fingerprint also records everything a later
.BR osfp\-compare (1)
needs to scan the same perimeter: the root, the exclusions, the mount policy
and the depth limit.
The command line of
.B compare
cannot change them.
.PP
The file is written under a temporary name and renamed once it is complete
and synced, so a file under the final name is always whole.
It is created with mode 0600: it lists every path on the system.
.PP
When it is done,
.B baseline
prints a summary: the key, the scope, the entries by type, what was hashed,
what was skipped, and every mount point it did not cross, with its
filesystem type.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " FILE"
Write the fingerprint to
.IR FILE .
The default is
.IB key .osfp
in the current directory, where
.I key
is the fingerprint key of the running system; see
.BR osfp (1).
.TP
.B \-\-force
Overwrite
.I FILE
if it exists.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " PATH"
Leave
.I PATH
out of the fingerprint.
Repeatable.
See
.B EXCLUSIONS
for the pattern syntax.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude\-from " FILE"
Read exclusion patterns from
.IR FILE ,
one per line.
Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-default\-excludes
Start from an empty exclusion list instead of the built\-in one.
.TP
.BI \-\-root " DIR"
Scan
.I DIR
instead of the whole filesystem.
An exclusion that would cover
.I DIR
itself is dropped, and a note on standard error says which.
.TP
.BI \-\-mounts " POLICY"
What to do at a mount point; see
.BR "MOUNT POINTS" .
.I POLICY
is
.B local
(the default),
.B same
or
.BR all .
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-depth " N"
Do not descend more than
.I N
levels below the root.
The default is 64, a guard against recursive bind mounts.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-file\-size " N"
Do not hash files larger than
.I N
bytes; record their size and modification time instead.
.BR osfp\-compare (1)
then falls back to those two for these files alone, and reports a difference
as a change of metadata, never of content.
.TP
.BI \-\-jobs " N"
Hash with
.I N
workers.
The default is the number of CPUs, at most 8.
.TP
.BI \-\-os\-id " KEY"
Use
.I KEY
as the fingerprint key instead of the detected one, for a system
.B osfp
does not recognise.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-non\-root
Scan without root privileges.
The fingerprint is marked UNPRIVILEGED, and can only be compared by a scan
that is unprivileged too.
Meant for tests.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Report progress on standard error, at most once a second: entries seen, bytes
hashed, time elapsed.
On a terminal the line is redrawn in place and erased at the end; redirected,
each update is a line of its own.
.SH EXCLUSIONS
An exclusion leaves a path out of the fingerprint entirely.
A pattern is one of:
.TP
.I /absolute/path
the path and everything below it;
.TP
.I /absolute/*/glob
a glob matched against the whole path;
.TP
.I name
or
.I *.glob
a glob matched against the base name, at any depth.
.PP
The built\-in list, recorded in the fingerprint with the rest, is
.BR /proc ,
.BR /sys ,
.BR /dev ,
.BR /tmp ,
.BR /var/tmp ,
.BR /run ,
.BR /var/run ,
.BR /media ,
.B /mnt
and
.BR /lost+found ,
plus, on Linux,
.B /var/lib/docker
and
.BR /swapfile ;
on FreeBSD,
.B /compat/linux/proc
and
.BR /var/db/freebsd\-update ;
on Solaris and illumos,
.BR /devices ,
.BR /system/contract ,
.BR /system/object ,
.B /system/volatile
and
.BR /rpool ;
on macOS,
.BR /Volumes ,
.BR /System/Volumes ,
.BR /private/var/vm ,
.B /.Spotlight\-V100
and
.BR /.fseventsd .
.PP
.B /home
and
.B /root
are deliberately not excluded: what appears there after installation is what
an audit is looking for.
.PP
The binary itself and the fingerprint being written are excluded
automatically.
.SH MOUNT POINTS
.TP
.B local
Cross into another local filesystem; stop at pseudo filesystems (procfs,
devfs, tmpfs, autofs and the like) and network ones (NFS, SMB, and on
Solaris and illumos lofs).
The type is read with
.BR statfs (2)
or
.BR statvfs (2);
on AIX it is a number, named from
.IR /etc/vfs .
A filesystem whose type cannot be determined is not crossed.
.TP
.B same
Stop at every mount point.
.TP
.B all
Cross everything, network shares included.
.PP
The default is
.BR local ,
not
.BR same :
on a ZFS, btrfs or LVM installation
.BR /home ,
.BR /usr ,
.B /var
and
.B /var/log
are often separate filesystems, and stopping at every mount point would leave
most of the system out.
Whatever is not crossed is named in the summary, with its type.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# osfp baseline \-o /root/install.osfp \-v
# osfp baseline \-\-exclude /srv \-\-exclude\-from site\-excludes.txt
# osfp baseline \-\-root /usr \-o usr.osfp
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR osfp (1),
.BR osfp\-compare (1)
